  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "What is the index of the term $-62$ for the arithmetic sequence below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=30,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that $f(n)=-62.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+d.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d=-4.$ We are also told that $f(1)=30,$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =30+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =30-4n+4 \\\\  & =34-4n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that $f(n)=-62:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =34-4n \\\\ -62 & =34-4n \\\\ -96 & =-4n \\\\ \\frac{-96}{-4} & =n \\\\ 24 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-62$ is the $24$th term of the sequence.",
